out exactly how you need  to run your business exactly what you've  got to employ uh pay your employees  exactly uh what health and safety  measures you need to take on the stairs  or the production line or is it  relatively light touch those are some  indications  of the metrics they use and the idea  behind this report is to find out really  whether adopting across those 43 indices  pro freedom approaches and policies  is the it brings with it successful  outcomes so what we can do is to compare  basically  the most classical liberal the most  capitalist you might say economies and  to compare that information to see if  those economies have better outcomes for  their citizens compared to what you  might call more socialist more  interventionist economies with a much  bigger state  and less role for the individual um  so  to try and enable that that analysis to  take place  uh to see if there is a correlation  between economic freedom classical  liberalism and human well-being uh the  this index uh the economic freedom of  the world index uh splits the 162  economies into quartiles into four  groups  based on their level of economic freedom  a bit like i guess our four football  divisions in england this is a premier  league uh going all the way down to the  league two or the fourth division as it  used to be said and they'll put  the fraser institute will put the most  classical liberal societies in the  premier league  and the least liberal and free societies  in league two and then we can compare  these different groups are the more  socialist economies doing well or the  um  or the uh more liberal classical liberal  free market economies doing well  and the results in my view are so  staggering that even if you have  complaints about the exact methodology  the evidence is overwhelming and the  jury is in  if you want the people in your society  to be  healthy wealthy wise  happy insofar as that can be measured  then classical liberalism free market  capitalism is unambiguously uh the way  forward and the tools that you should  adopt so let me give you some of those  statistics without just i don't want to  machine gun you with endless numbers but  let me talk through some of them so i've  made a lot of assertions here but i  haven't made any i haven't given you any  proof let's look at uh  average incomes  uh income's not the only thing that  matters to people but i guess it matters  quite a lot something that we discuss a  lot we get worried if incomes in britain  are going down which clearly they are at  the moment in the lockdown uh but let's  look at the average income in that uh  top quartile of countries and by top i  mean the most classically liberal  and compare it to the 25 percent of  countries that are the least free they  have the highest taxes the biggest state  the highest regulation the worst rule of  law and the rest of it and what you find  is in the  uh freer countries the most classically  liberal ones average incomes are six  times higher so in those top 25  countries from a classical liberal  perspective the average income is  a little bit higher than 36 000 u.s  dollars  and in the worst 25 that bottom division  the average income is just a little  above 6 000 us dollars so there's a  remarkable  stunning correlation at least between  being classical liberal broadly  capitalist in free market and having  high average  incomes  uh but what's interesting as well  because i can anticipate a question will  be oh yeah well it might be the rich  benefit but what about the poor uh if  you're averaging it out that might just  be because there's lots of billionaires  and millionaires but the people at the  bottom of a capitalist free market  classical liberal society suffering far  more than they would in a socialist  society no the gap here is even more  staggering so if you look at the bottom  10  of income earners in different countries  the lowest 10  and you look at the lowest 10 percent  in  capitalist countries classical liberal  countries and the lowest 10  in the least classically liberal  countries then actually you the the  difference is seven times higher income  in the richer countries so if you're  going to be in the bottom 10  of the country that you live in  uh you still definitely definitely want  to be living in a relatively free market  liberal capitalist country not in a  socialist one even more so than the  average earner if you're an average  earner you appear to be six times better  off earning six times as much money  as in the more socialist societies but  if you're on that bottom ten percent  you're seven times better off than you  would be if you were in the bottom 10 of  a poorer society um  similarly if you were looking at not  just the bottom 10 but extreme absolute  adjective poverty i think this is  defined by the world bank of trying to  get by on a salary of or income of less  than uh  1.90 a day  uh that's reckoned to be about the point  that you're probably below subsistence  uh you know nearly all you know one hell  of a challenge to  stay alive so how many people are uh  struggling on those appalling uh income  levels well again if you look at the  fourth division the least classically  liberal countries about 27  of people in those countries are living  in that extreme poverty in utter  destitution more than a quarter  struggling to even live  and if you look at the most classically  liberal economies those with relatively  lower taxes less regulation and less of  a role for a state there is still abject  poverty but it's a bit less than two  percent of the population who live in  the freer countries so what we see  pretty clearly if you like almost  whichever group you wish to compare with  whichever group whether you want to  compare the average citizen in  relatively in free market countries to  the average citizen in socialist  countries whether you want to compare  the bottom 10 of the citizenry with the  bottom 10 percent of the citizenry or  whether you actually want to look at how  many you know how good is each society  at making sure that people aren't living  in absolute devastating poverty then  there is no doubt you want to be in that  classical liberal free-market catalyst  league of clubs  you want to be low tax low regulation  low government spending  uh good quality rule of law and the rest  uh but it's not just about economics uh  you'll probably be unsurprised to know  that the other metrics that we might  care about are um  produce better results in classical  liberal countries than in their  socialist counterparts uh so  if you want to live longer uh well you  want to be in that top quartile of the  most free market countries in the world  you will on average uh live 14 years  longer than someone who is in the least  classical liberal quartile that fourth  division of countries  uh  other metrics you might want to look at  i'll say infant mortality that top  quartile of countries the most classical  liberal  their infant mortality rate is six times  lower than the bottom 25 percent the  most socialist countries  and you can look at a whole range of  other  measures as well if you like  non-economic factors that you might  consider have real value and you'll find  that the  most classical liberal countries have  far greater political and civil  liberties greater gender equality  and the extent to which you think you  can measure things and this is a hazy  area seem to indicate that they're  happier countries  as well  so it seems to me that the case is uh  pretty overwhelmingly  strong that uh if you want those things  longer life less  less people dying in child death higher  incomes not just for the affluent but  for the average the poor and a lack of  destitution the the the evidence is in  according to the fraser institute you  want to be one of the classical liberal  countries  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description bel